! An oxygen **molecule** at fixed occupations with nspin = 2, which is the
! spin-polarized insulator the sliced branch of the Sternheimer solver needs and
! `o-atom-fixed-lsda.in` cannot be.
!
! O2 is a triplet: twelve valence electrons, tot_magnetization = 2, so
! nelup = 7 and neldw = 5. Both of those are **closed shells** --
! (2sigma_g, 2sigma_u, 3sigma_g, 1pi_u x2, 1pi_g* x2) up and the same list
! without the pi* down -- so each channel has a real gap above its last occupied
! band and the two channels are occupied to *different* depths. That pair is the
! whole point: it is the case a single `nelec / 2` gets wrong, and the case a
! degenerate cut does not spoil.
!
! The oxygen *atom* of `o-atom-fixed-lsda.in` is the counterexample and is why
! this file exists. There nelup = 4 and neldw = 2, and the minority channel's
! second electron sits in one member of the triply degenerate 2p shell: the
! filling cuts a multiplet, `H - eps_n S` is singular along the empty partners,
! and the Sternheimer CG has nothing to converge to. That case is refused by
! name (`sternheimer.DEGENERATE_CUT_RY`); this one runs.
!
! Gamma only, written as an explicit k = 0 so that both codes use the full
! sphere rather than the gamma-point trick. The box is a molecule-in-a-cell
! rather than an isolated molecule -- what is being checked is that two routes
! to the same response agree, not that the box is converged.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 1, celldm(1) = 10.0,
    nat = 2, ntyp = 1,
    nbnd = 9,
    ecutwfc = 25.0, ecutrho = 200.0,
    occupations = 'fixed',
    nspin = 2,
    tot_magnetization = 2,
    starting_magnetization(1) = 0.5
 /
 &electrons
    mixing_beta = 0.3
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 O  15.99994  O.pz-rrkjus.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (bohr)
 O 0.00 0.00 0.00
 O 0.00 0.00 2.30
K_POINTS tpiba
 1
 0.0 0.0 0.0 1.0
